"""Report exact finite-control information separately from neural/system evidence."""
import json,csv
import numpy as np
from .information_budget import OUT,sha

def main():
    p=json.loads((OUT/'protocol.json').read_text());r=json.loads((OUT/'statistics.json').read_text());a=json.loads((OUT/'evidence_audit.json').read_text());assert a['passed'] and a['producer_manifest_sha256']==sha(OUT/'manifest.json')
    for source,h in p['source_sha256'].items():assert sha(source)==h
    rows=r['rows'];fields=sorted({k for row in rows for k in row})
    with (OUT/'metrics.csv').open('w',newline='') as f:writer=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=fields);writer.writeheader();writer.writerows(rows)
    lines=['# What a p-bit trajectory reveals about its programmed weight','','A p-bit\'s instantaneous state is binary. The programmed stationary probability is a separate control setting. This exact channel calculation quantifies how repeated independent observations reveal that setting. It uses the same finite F32 fields and official unary mapping p=σ(2b) already checked by native THRML experiments. No new native samples, model scores or device measurements are taken.','','For a discrete control C with priorπ and T Bernoulli observations, the count N is sufficient: conditional on N, all sequences with that count are equiprobable independently of C. Thus I(C;states)=I(C;N), bounded by min(H(C),log2(T+1)). The first term counts distinguishable programmed settings; the second limits information extracted from the trajectory. Information in bits is not ENOB or a neural quality metric.','','The uniform256-code prior covers all8-bit field settings uniformly; it is not a uniform weight prior or the observed distribution of neural controls. A second, matched16-level prior assigns equal mass to desired weights0,1/15,...,1 for both one programmable p-bit and four positional p-bits. One-node probabilities use the finite8-bit field grid; four-node words use +/-B finite fields for desired bit labels. The latter has independent binary labels, so information sums across bits. Both endpoints remain stochastic, not clamped.','','## Matched16-level scalar prior at equal node updates','','| B | Total updates | One-node information (bits) | Four-node information (bits) | One-node Bayes MMSE | Four-node Bayes MMSE |','|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
    for b in [4,2]:
        for updates in [4,16,64,256,1024,4096]:
            one=next(v for v in rows if v['model']=='single16' and v['field_bound']==b and v['samples']==updates);multi=next(v for v in rows if v['model']=='positional4' and v['field_bound']==b and v['node_updates']==updates);lines.append(f'| {b} | {updates} | {one["mutual_information_bits"]:.6f} | {multi["mutual_information_bits"]:.6f} | {one["Bayes_MMSE"]:.8g} | {multi["Bayes_MMSE"]:.8g} |')
    lines+=['','Bayes MMSE assumes the exact finite desired-weight prior and an optimal posterior-mean decoder. It is a theoretical lower risk under that prior, not the sample-frequency decoder used in native neural operators, a deployed decoder, or a way to avoid accumulation/readout costs. No neural improvement is claimed from these analytic rows.','','## Eight-bit control code information','','| B | Samples | Information about8-bit control (bits) | Bayes MMSE for programmed p | Unbiased frequency MSE |','|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
    for b in [4,2]:
        for v in [z for z in rows if z['model']=='single256' and z['field_bound']==b]:lines.append(f'| {b} | {v["samples"]} | {v["mutual_information_bits"]:.6f} | {v["Bayes_MMSE"]:.8g} | {v["unbiased_frequency_MSE"]:.8g} |')
    lines+=['','Different code priors and target alphabets cannot be treated as equal task quality. The8-bit setting entropy does not imply eight physical digital cells: analog retention/control circuits require hardware characterization. It also does not mean an instantaneous binary state stores the entire floating weight independently of that setting. Finite controls bound distinguishable probabilities; sampling adds an extraction cost. A continuously programmable ideal bias is a different model and does not acquire arbitrary precision from one observed bit.','','All saved binomial likelihoods were independently reconstructed by mode-centered recurrences, rather than the producer\'s SciPy PMF routine. Mutual information was checked by the KL formula rather than entropy subtraction; Bayes risk by posterior squared deviations rather than a second-moment difference. Every information bound and monotonicity check passed. These are F64 numerical calculations, not symbolic infinite-precision certificates. Full raw rows include2/4/8-node positional words; plots and tables do not establish physical energy, latency, area or superiority over Extropic. Earlier simulator and neural-quality outcomes remain unchanged. The long-horizon goal stays active.']
    path=OUT.parent/'INFORMATION_BUDGET_REPORT.md';path.write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
    import matplotlib;matplotlib.use('Agg');import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
    fig,axes=plt.subplots(1,2,figsize=(11,4.5),layout='constrained')
    for ax,b in zip(axes,[4,2]):
        for model,label in [('single256','One node,256 equiprobable controls'),('single16','One node,16 desired weights'),('positional4','Four nodes,16 desired weights')]:
            rr=sorted([z for z in rows if z['field_bound']==b and z['model']==model],key=lambda z:z['node_updates']);ax.semilogx([z['node_updates'] for z in rr],[z['mutual_information_bits'] for z in rr],'o-',label=label)
        ax.axhline(4,color='gray',linestyle=':');ax.axhline(8,color='gray',linestyle=':');ax.set(xlabel='Stochastic node observations/updates',ylabel='Information about finite control label (bits)',title=f'Exact IID channel; field bound B={b}',ylim=(0,8.4));ax.legend(fontsize=7)
    fig.savefig(OUT.parent/'information_budget.png',dpi=180);plt.close(fig);print(path)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
